                <h2>Comprehensive Skin Cancer Care</h2>
                <p>Skin cancer is the most common form of cancer in the United States, but it's also one of the most treatable when caught early. At Smart Skin Dermatology, our board-certified dermatologists have decades of experience in detecting and treating all types of skin cancer.</p>
                
                <h3>Types of Skin Cancer We Treat</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC):</strong> The most common type, typically appearing as a pearly or waxy bump, often on sun-exposed areas</li>
                    <li><strong>Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC):</strong> Often appears as a firm, red nodule or flat lesion with a scaly surface</li>
                    <li><strong>Melanoma:</strong> The most serious form, often appearing as a mole that changes in size, shape, or color</li>
                    <li><strong>Actinic Keratoses:</strong> Pre-cancerous lesions that can develop into squamous cell carcinoma</li>
                    <li><strong>Merkel Cell Carcinoma:</strong> A rare but aggressive form of skin cancer</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>The ABCDE Rule for Melanoma Detection</h3>
                <p>Watch for these warning signs in moles:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>A - Asymmetry:</strong> One half doesn't match the other</li>
                    <li><strong>B - Border:</strong> Irregular, ragged, or blurred edges</li>
                    <li><strong>C - Color:</strong> Multiple colors or uneven distribution</li>
                    <li><strong>D - Diameter:</strong> Larger than 6mm (pencil eraser size)</li>
                    <li><strong>E - Evolving:</strong> Changes in size, shape, or color over time</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Our Skin Cancer Screening Process</h3>
                <p>During your full-body skin exam, we will:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Systematically examine all areas of your skin</li>
                    <li>Use dermoscopy to examine suspicious lesions in detail</li>
                    <li>Document and monitor any concerning spots</li>
                    <li>Perform biopsies of suspicious lesions as needed</li>
                    <li>Discuss your individual risk factors and prevention strategies</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Skin Cancer Treatment Options</h3>
                <p>Treatment depends on the type, size, location, and depth of the cancer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Surgical Excision:</strong> Removing the cancer along with a margin of healthy tissue</li>
                    <li><strong>Mohs Surgery:</strong> Precise layer-by-layer removal for complex or high-risk cancers (referred to specialists)</li>
                    <li><strong>Curettage & Electrodesiccation:</strong> Scraping and burning for superficial cancers</li>
                    <li><strong>Cryotherapy:</strong> Freezing pre-cancerous and some early cancerous lesions</li>
                    <li><strong>Topical Treatments:</strong> Prescription creams for precancers and superficial cancers</li>
                    <li><strong>Photodynamic Therapy:</strong> Light-activated treatment for certain skin cancers</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Who Should Get Screened?</h3>
                <p>We recommend annual skin cancer screenings for:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Anyone with a history of skin cancer</li>
                    <li>People with a family history of melanoma</li>
                    <li>Those with many moles or atypical moles</li>
                    <li>Fair-skinned individuals</li>
                    <li>People with a history of sunburns or tanning bed use</li>
                    <li>Those with weakened immune systems</li>
                    <li>Anyone noticing changes in existing moles or new skin growths</li>
                </ul>
